[2026-09-25T13:23:52] QUERY 3: Is there a technique that re-runs a whole computation from the beginning, guided by a log of earlier results, to get back to a paused point? How did its speed compare with a logic-programming language on the queens puzzle?
retrieved=
    0.500  StatusQuo: Making Familiar Abstractions Perform Using Program Analysis  [DBLP_conf_cidr_CheungAMSM13.pdf]
    0.500  Capturing the Future by Replaying the Past Functional Pearl  [DBLP_journals_corr_abs-1710-10385.pdf]
    0.500  Capturing the Future by Replaying the Past Functional Pearl  [DBLP_journals_pacmpl_KoppelSS18.pdf]
    0.488  Capturing the Future by Replaying the Past Functional Pearl  [DBLP_journals_pacmpl_KoppelSS18.pdf]
    0.485  Capturing the Future by Replaying the Past Functional Pearl  [DBLP_journals_corr_abs-1710-10385.pdf]
answer=Yes. The document describes a replay-based technique where “the whole computation [is] replayed” and “it restarts the whole computation u with future P+1, exactly like our implementation.”

On speed for the queens benchmark, it says the replay-based approach was competitive with logic programming: “optimized thermometers are just as fast as replay-nondeterminism.” It also says the benchmark compared against “GNU Prolog 1.4.4,” and that “the optimizations described in Section 6 suffice to remove the additional overhead.”

So, according to the document, the replay-based method could re-run the computation from the beginning using recorded history, and after optimization it was “just as fast” as the replay-nondeterminism approach on N-queens.
